et $[a X b]$[a X b] be a single-piece Inverse Treblecross state, with $a$a and $b$b denoting the lengths on either side of the piece, and let $\\mathcal{G}$G denote its Sprague–Grundy value. Asymptotic periodicity conjecture. For any fixed $a$a, the SG sequence of $[a X b]$[a X b] with respect to $b$b is eventually periodic with period $10$10. There exists a minimal preperiod $b_a$b a​ such that, for all $b>b_a$b>b a​,\n\n$$\\mathcal{G}([a X (b+5)])=\\mathcal{G}([a X b])\\oplus 1.$$G([a X(b+5)])=G([a X b])⊕1.\n\nMoreover,\n\n$$b_a=\\begin{cases}5,&a\\in\\{1,4\\};\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$b a​={5,0,​a∈{1,4};otherwise.​\n\nThis conjecture is motivated by computed single-piece SG values and by the isolated anomalies identified for the exceptional residue classes; the asserted eventual periodicity and exact preperiod bound remain open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nKai Liang and Muxi Li, “A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross”, arXiv:2604.16759 (2026).\n\ncombinatorial-game-theorysprague-grundy-theory--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeminars: Li MuXi, A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross: Periodicity, Characteristic Functions, and Teaching Practice (https://www.mathnet.ru/php/seminars.phtml?option_lang=eng&presentid=50540)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross: Periodicity, Characteristic Functions, and Teaching Practice\n\nMoscow-Beijing Topology seminar\nJune 3, 2026 10:30–12:00  \n\nA Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross: Periodicity, Characteristic Functions, and Teaching Practice\nLi MuXi^{}\nNumber of views:\nThis page: 208\nAbstract: This talk delves into the mathematical solving of an 1D impartial avoidance game we named \"Inverse Treblecross\", adapted from the game \"Treblecross\" introduced by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy in their famous work \"Winning Ways for Your Mathematical Plays\". For audiences new to Combinatorial Game Theory, the presentation will begin with a gentle introduction to the Sprague-Grundy (SG) theorem and octal games. We will see how standard games neatly decompose into independent subgames. However, Inverse Treblecross is notoriously stubborn: placing a piece does not cleanly isolate the remaining empty spaces, making it remarkably difficult to evaluate. Despite this resistance, we reveal a surprising hidden order. By constructing specific characteristic functions and mapping board parities, we demonstrate that the SG sequence for an empty board is ultimately confined to the values 0 and 1, perfectly dictated by a modulo-10 rule. In general, the topic of combinatorial game theory also plays an important role in mathematics education. I will conclude the talk by sharing how relevant topics originated and evolved through teaching practices. No prior knowledge of Combinatorial Game Theory is required. The talk will be self-contained and accessible to a general math audience.\nLanguage: English\nWebsite: https://us02web.zoom.us/j/81866745751?pwd=bEFqUUlZM1hVV0tvN0xWdXRsV2pnQT09--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeneralized reflectional periodicity conjecture for odd Inverse k-cross games | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/374252/generalized-reflectional-periodicity-conjecture-for-odd-inve)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; # Generalized reflectional periodicity conjecture for odd Inverse k-cross games ... Kai Liang and Muxi Li, “A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross”, arXiv:2604.16759 (2026).\n\n# Generalized reflectional periodicity conjecture for odd Inverse k-cross games\n\nLess than 1 year old · traced to 2026\n\nLet $k=2m+1$k=2 m+1 with $m\\geq1$m≥1, and consider an Inverse $k$k-cross game with empty-board state $[a]$[a]. Let $\\mathcal{G}([a])$G([a]) denote its Sprague–Grundy value, and let $\\oplus$⊕ denote nim-sum. Generalized reflectional periodicity conjecture. For every such game, $\\mathcal{G}([a])\\leq1$G([a])≤1, and\n\n$$\\mathcal{G}([2+3m+a])=\\mathcal{G}([a])\\oplus(m\\bmod2),$$G([2+3 m+a])=G([a])⊕(m mod 2), $$\\mathcal{G}([2+5m-a])=\\mathcal{G}([a])\\oplus(m\\bmod2).$$G([2+5 m−a])=G([a])⊕(m mod 2).\n\nFor $k=2$k=2, eventual periodicity is known through the identification with Dawson's Kayles, whereas for even $k>2$k>2 eventual periodicity is unknown; the asserted uniform bound and reflectional periodicity for odd constraints remain conjectural.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Kai Liang and Muxi Li, “A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross”, arXiv:2604.16759 (2026).\n\ncombinatorial-game-theorysprague-grundy-theory--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nresearchseminars.org - View talk (https://researchseminars.org/talk/Mos-Bei-top-seminar/145/)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross: Periodicity, Characteristic Functions, and Teaching Practice\n\n# A Weak Solution of Inverse Treblecross: Periodicity, Characteristic Functions, and Teaching Practice\n\n### Muxi Li\n\nWed Jun 3, 07:30-09:00 (5 months ago)\n\nAbstract: This talk delves into the mathematical solving of an 1D impartial avoidance game we named \"Inverse Treblecross\", adapted from the game \"Treblecross\" introduced by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy in their famous work \"Winning Ways for Your Mathematical Plays\". For audiences new to Combinatorial Game Theory, the presentation will begin with a gentle introduction to the Sprague-Grundy (SG) theorem and octal games. We will see how standard games neatly decompose into independent subgames. However, Inverse Treblecross is notoriously stubborn: placing a piece does not cleanly isolate the remaining empty spaces, making it remarkably difficult to evaluate. Despite this resistance, we reveal a surprising hidden order. By constructing specific characteristic functions and mapping board parities, we demonstrate that the SG sequence for an empty board is ultimately c